## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical E2E testing skills around feature flags, data seeding, cross-device validation, and deterministic mocks. It also evaluates how candidates handle paralellization, event deduplication, and rollback correctness in a live-like rollout.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flags and gradual rollouts
- Deterministic data seeding for stable tests
- Mocking external services (flag service, analytics)
- Cross-device (desktop/mobile) parity and isolation
- Event deduplication and correct rollback behavior

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright skeleton for ranking_v2 on/off
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test('ranking_v2 enabled shows new ranking and analytics', async ({ page, request }) => {
  // seed data and mock flag service to return true for ranking_v2
  // navigate to search and verify ordering matches ranking_v2
  // assert analytics endpoint received a single event per click
});
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate rollout skew and handle hotfix rollbacks?
- How would you scale this test to 1000+ products with performance constraints?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a beginner-friendly E2E scenario involving asynchronous background work, multiple data stores, and end-to-end verification of UI and backend state.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end vs component tests
- Asynchronous processing and polling
- Test data seeding and isolation
- Mocking external services with determinism
- Retry and failure handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch (Playwright/Cypress style)
// Seed user/feed
seedUser({ id: 'u1' });
// Upload
uploadImage('/path/photo.jpg');
// Mock storage to return fixed URLs
mockStorageUrls({ original: 'https://storage/img/orig.jpg', s1: 'https://storage/img/s1.jpg', s2: 'https://storage/img/s2.jpg', s3: 'https://storage/img/s3.jpg' });
// Poll for processing completion
waitForProcessing('image-upload-u1', 60000);
// Verify feed shows three sizes
verifyFeedItemHasSizes(['orig','s1','s2','s3']);
// Verify search index entry
verifyIndexEntry({ id: 'img-u1', urls: ['orig','s1','s2','s3'] });
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle flaky network conditions during the image upload in this test?
- How would you extend this to verify eventual consistency if the index updates survive a restart?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for per-user feature flags are common in SaaS apps. This question checks the ability to seed data, perform API-driven state changes, and verify UI behavior across login and page reloads in a beginner-friendly way.

## Key Concepts
- Per-user feature flags and API seed/reset
- End-to-end UI verification of dynamic layouts
- Test isolation and deterministic CI runs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example using Playwright-like syntax (pseudocode)
// seedUser(), setFlag(), login() are helper utilities
test('compact dashboard toggling', async ({ page, request }) => {
  const { userId, email, password } = await seedUser(request);
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', true);
  await login(page, email, password);
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-compact')).toBeVisible();
  await setFlag(request, userId, 'CompactDashboard', false);
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('.dashboard-full')).toBeVisible();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you guard against flaky UI assertions caused by caching?
- How would you extend the test to cover multiple user segments (roles/regions) and parallel execution?

